  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 AM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\n06z surface analysis depicts a cold front moving southeastward \nacross the forecast area early this morning. This feature is a\nmuch welcoming change to the relentless heat the area has been\nexperiencing as of late. While temperatures will be still\nslightly above average for the beginning of September, most\nareas will remain in the upper 80s to low/mid 90s. Depending on\nexactly the placement of the front through the afternoon, 100 is\nstill possible in south-central Kansas. While low-level\nmoisture will still be elevated, the lower temperatures should\nkeep us from reaching widespread Heat Advisory heat index\ncriteria. Thursday will be the day that will feel noticeably\nbetter than recent days. High temperatures across the entire\nforecast area are expected to remain in the 80s. It may feel\nslightly hotter with dew points remaining elevated once again,\nbut any max heat indices are expected to only perhaps touch the\nlow 90s.\n\nWidespread thunderstorm chances will increase late this\nafternoon, this evening, and tonight across a large part of the\nforecast area. The aforementioned cold front will stall along \nor near the KS/OK border. Given abundant low-level moisture on \nthe north and south side of the boundary, numerous showers and \nthunderstorms are expected. Early in the event (during the late\nafternoon and early evening), an isolated severe weather risk \nmay materialize. With a deep mixing temperature and dew point \nprofile, leading to steep low-level lapse rates and DCAPEs \naround 1200-1500 J/kg, some strong downburst potential does \nexist. However, given weak mid-level flow, only a few loosely \norganized multi-cell clusters are probable. So overall severe \nweather potential will be minimal, as a Marginal Risk is in \nplace to reflect this potential. As more widespread storms \ndevelop into the evening and overnight hours, the hazard will \ntransition into more of a heavy rainfall risk. Latest NBM \nprobabilities of widespread 0.25\" or greater is 30-60%, mainly \nalong and south of Route 50 corridor. However, latest CAMs have \nbegun to pick up on the potential for some training of \nthunderstorms during the evening and into the overnight hours, \nwith very localized 1-2\"+ totals a possibility. Again, similar \nto the severe risk, any hydro issues will be limited and not \nwidespread. However, a few areas may experience at least some \nnuisance flooding.\n\nLooking into this weekend and beyond, the ridge that has had a \ngrip on the area as broken down and more troughs are expected to\nsweep into the Plains. With the exception of a day or two \nthrown in with hotter temperatures, temperatures will largely be\nkept at bay. MaxT forecast stay in the 80s and 90s through \nweekend and into next week. With these approaching troughs, some\nPOPs (20-40%) are in the forecast early next week.",
